# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here — Kube-OVN is
# platform infrastructure installed once per cluster (ADR-019) and what this chart renders is a
# tenant's row in that installation — and `vendored: none` is an answer to "where did this come from"
# where a missing file is not.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/kubeovn/kube-ovn

# The upstream API this chart's template targets. ⚠ An API group/version, not a commit: nothing is
# vendored, so there is no upstream tree to pin.
upstream-api: kubeovn.io/v1

engine: https://github.com/ovn-org/ovn

See ../kube-ovn-vpc/SOURCE for the family's shared review of the Kube-OVN CRD set and the
controller's habit of writing back to `.spec`. What was read at tag v1.16.2 for THIS chart, and the
facts that bear hardest on it:

  * pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/ovn-eip.go —
    `+kubebuilder:resource:scope="Cluster",shortName="oeip",path="ovn-eips",singular="ovn-eip"`.
    CLUSTER-SCOPED and the plural is HYPHENATED, which is the same trap security-groups carried:
    ClusterConformanceHarness derives its CRD stub's path from GroupVersionKind.Plural.
  * OvnEipSpec is five OPTIONAL strings — externalSubnet, v4Ip, v6Ip, macAddress, type. Checked in
    the CRD the chart installs: `spec` declares NO `required:` list and NO `default:` for any member,
    so a spec carrying only the fields this chart means is a valid object.
  * pkg/controller/ovn_eip.go `createOrUpdateOvnEipCR` issues a full `OvnEips().Update(...)` that
    writes spec.macAddress, spec.v4Ip, spec.v6Ip and spec.type — THE CONTROLLER WRITES BACK TO
    `.spec`, which is this family's third defaulting mechanism and the one it keeps meeting. It is
    why Matches is containment and why this chart emits `v4Ip`/`v6Ip` only when a tenant asked for a
    particular address: an applied `v4Ip: ""` claims a field the controller then sets to the
    allocated address, and every later apply answers a field-manager conflict.
  * `handleAddOvnEip` falls back to `c.config.ExternalGatewaySwitch` when spec.externalSubnet is
    empty. That flag is `--external-gateway-switch`, its default is "external" (pkg/controller/
    config.go) and it exists so a deployment can name its provider bridge something else — so this
    chart does NOT send the pool. ⚠ THE OPPOSITE OF THE SUBNET CHART'S `spec.vpc` DECISION, and
    deliberately: there the substrate's default is the platform's own VPC and is wrong, here it is
    the operator's own pool and is the only right answer available.
  * ⚠ AND THE FIELD IS **NOT** WRITTEN BACK on an object this platform applied, which is easy to
    assume and wrong. `createOrUpdateOvnEipCR`'s update branch touches macAddress, v4Ip, v6Ip and
    type and leaves externalSubnet alone; only its CREATE branch — for EIPs the controller makes
    itself — sets it. The pool the fabric chose is observable on the `ovn.kubernetes.io/subnet` label
    the same function writes, which is a different label namespace from ADR-013's seven.
  * ⚠ THE HAZARD THAT ARGUMENT HAD TO SURVIVE, CHECKED: both delete paths call
    `c.ipam.ReleaseAddressByPod(eip.Name, eip.Spec.ExternalSubnet)` with the field this chart leaves
    empty, which reads like an address that is never returned to the pool. pkg/ipam/ipam.go branches
    on it — a non-empty name releases from that subnet, an EMPTY name releases from EVERY subnet. So
    the empty field is a superset rather than a no-op and nothing leaks.
  * `handleUpdateOvnEip` refuses a changed v4Ip, v6Ip, macAddress and type, one error each, every one
    beginning "not support change"; `handleAddOvnEip` returns early once status.macAddress is set. AN
    OvnEip IS WHOLLY IMMUTABLE ONCE READY. conformance.yaml § owed,
    `an-allocated-address-cannot-be-changed`.
  * The deletion path in `handleUpdateOvnEip` refuses to drop the finalizer while a NAT rule names
    the address — "is still being used by NAT rules … waiting for them to be deleted" — so a delete
    can legitimately not finish. Nothing can attach an address in this api-version, so it cannot
    happen yet.
  * OvnEipStatus carries v4Ip, v6Ip, macAddress, ready, nat and conditions[]. `POST …/showAllocation`
    reads exactly those; the allocated address exists NOWHERE ELSE.
